<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsam al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 963–998</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abu Kalijar Marzuban</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsam al-Dawla was a Buyid emir, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>, who succeeded his father in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 983. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-dawla">Sharaf al-Dawla</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> from him in 987 and had him imprisoned and partly blinded. Released after Sharaf's death, he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> from 989 until 998, when he was killed in a revolt led by sons of his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samsam-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Samsam al-Dawla" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samsam_al-Dawla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
